          "description": "Compare the cost advantage of water-based mud",
          "required": true,
          "support_status": "SUPPORTED",
          "supporting_evidence_ids": [
            "KB2"
          ],
          "supporting_spans": [
            "Water based muds are relatively inexpensive because of the ready supply of the fluid \nfrom which they are made - water."
          ]
        },
        {
          "claim_id": "CLAIM2",
          "description": "with its main shale-related risk",
          "required": true,
          "support_status": "SUPPORTED",
          "supporting_evidence_ids": [
            "KB1",
            "KB1",
            "KB1",
            "KB1"
          ],
          "supporting_spans": [
            "The main disadvantage of using water based muds is that the water in these muds \ncauses instability in shales.",
            "Shale is composed primarily of clays and instability is \nlargely caused by hydration of the clays by mud containing water.",
            "Shales are the most \ncommon rock types encountered while drilling for oil and gas and give rise to more \nproblems per meter drilled than any other type of formation.",
            "Estimates of worldwide, \nnonproductive costs associated with shale problems are put at $500 to $600 million \nannually (1997)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "claim_id": "CLAIM3",
          "description": "distinguish the roles of active clay solids",
          "required": true,
          "support_status": "SUPPORTED",
          "supporting_evidence_ids": [
            "KB2",
            "KB2"
          ],
          "supporting_spans": [
            "Some solids (clays) react with the water and chemicals in the \nmud and are called active solids.",
            "The activity of these solids must be controlled in \norder to allow the mud to function properly."
          ]
        },
        {
          "claim_id": "CLAIM4",
          "description": "inert barite",
          "required": true,
          "support_status": "SUPPORTED",
